## Summary

The name text was never actually *absent* — it was rendering the **wrong source**, and a merge bug could wipe the real name entirely.

**Root cause** — two defects in the data binding:

1. **Name-wipe bug** ([user-storage.ts:151](FrontEnd/src/app/lib/user-storage.ts#L151)) — `saveStoredUser` did a blind `{ ...current, ...user }` spread. When a server payload (e.g. `/api/users/profile`) returned `fullName: ""` or whitespace, it overwrote the good name already in local storage. On the next load the header fell back to the email-derived handle — which is exactly the "Mamamo099" you saw instead of the real name. This affected every caller (root-layout, profile, dashboard-layout, sign-in).
2. **Same clobber in the profile fetch** ([profile.tsx:685](FrontEnd/src/app/pages/profile.tsx#L685)) — `setLocalUser((prev) => ({ ...prev, ...res.user }))` merged the empty server name straight into component state.

**Fix** — added `pickName` / `preserveKnownName` in [user-storage.ts](FrontEnd/src/app/lib/user-storage.ts), which prefer the first non-empty value across `fullName → name → userName` (server, then local) and skip empty strings. Both `saveStoredUser` branches now use it, and the profile effect routes through `saveStoredUser` + `getStoredUser()` instead of a raw spread, so all merge paths are covered.

**Visibility** — the header `h1` at [profile.tsx:1466](FrontEnd/src/app/pages/profile.tsx#L1466) uses `text-foreground`, and `body` applies `text-foreground` over `bg-background`, so contrast is correct in both themes. Confirmed in dark mode (`oklch(0.985)` near-white on `oklch(0.145)` card).

**Verified**
- Guard unit test (fresh module): empty payload → keeps `"Maria Clara Santos"`; real payload → server wins; whitespace-only → no wipe. All pass.
- Browser repro of the original symptom, then dark-mode render after the fix — full name + correct `getInitials` (`MC`).
- `npm run build` exit 0.
- Seeded session data removed; my dev server and tab closed.

**Limitation:** the empty-`fullName` *end-to-end* path couldn't be exercised against the live API — all 5 DB accounts with empty `fullName` still have a `userName`, so the backend always resolves a non-empty name. The wipe path was therefore tested at the module level (the exact function the page now calls), not through a real HTTP response. No `tsconfig.json` exists in `FrontEnd/`, so there's no separate typecheck to run; `vite build` is the project's only check and it doesn't type-check.

Follow-ups worth considering:
- Add a `tsconfig.json` with `tsc --noEmit` to the build so type errors surface (right now nothing type-checks this frontend).
- Backfill the 5 DB accounts missing `fullName` from their `userName` so the server returns a real name instead of relying on the client-side fallback.
- The password-strength duplicate in [settings.tsx:66](FrontEnd/src/app/pages/settings.tsx#L66) still has its own scoring logic — could reuse the shared component.
